\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:methodology}

\subsection{Base Architecture}
\label{sec:base_arch}

All experiments build on DreamerV3 \citep{hafner2023dreamerv3}, which learns a recurrent state-space model (RSSM) with discrete latent states ($32$ categories $\times$ $32$ classes), uses symlog-transformed predictions, and trains an actor-critic entirely within imagined trajectories. The standard configuration imagines $H{=}15$ steps at a fixed temporal resolution of $\dt{=}1$, meaning each imagination step corresponds to one environment transition.

\subsection{Variable-Rate Imagination Mechanism}
\label{sec:variable_rate}

We introduce three orthogonal architectural modifications that enable variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3. Each can be independently toggled to isolate its contribution.

\paragraph{Temporal embedding.}
A learned embedding $e(\dt)$ is concatenated to the RSSM input at each imagination step to condition state predictions on the temporal scale. We test three modes:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Trained}: embedding parameters are updated via backpropagation through the world model loss, so the RSSM can co-adapt its dynamics and reward predictions to the temporal scale.
\item \textbf{Frozen}: a fixed random projection of identical dimensionality (\texttt{requires\_grad=False}). The RSSM receives a temporal signal but cannot co-adapt to it.
\item \textbf{Removed}: embedding dimension set to zero; no temporal conditioning signal is provided.
\end{itemize}
We further distinguish \textbf{trained-and-exposed} (subsample ON: the RSSM trains on $\dt{>}1$ transitions) from \textbf{trained-but-unexposed} (subsample OFF: $\dt{>}1$ used only during imagination). This four-way classification (trained/exposed, trained/unexposed, frozen, removed) is the key causal structure of our factorial design.

\paragraph{Temporal subsampling.}
When enabled, the replay buffer augments training data with variable-$\dt$ transitions by subsampling consecutive frames. Concretely, with a maximum subsampling factor of~2, the RSSM trains on both $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$ transitions drawn from the same episodes. When disabled, only standard $\dt{=}1$ transitions are used. This factor controls whether the world model has seen multi-scale dynamics during training.

\paragraph{Imagination horizon and step size.}
The imagination horizon $H$ sets the number of rollout steps. We test $H{=}15$ (default) and $H{=}8$. The reduced horizon controls for compounding error: $H{=}8$ steps at $\dt{=}2$ covers $16$ physical time steps, comparable to the $15$ physical steps of the default configuration. The parameter \texttt{gate\_fixed\_dt} forces all imagination steps to use a specific $\dt$ value ($1$, $2$, or $4$); this separates the effect of $\dt$-conditioning from any learned gating policy.

\subsection{Factorial Design}
\label{sec:factorial}

We adopt a structured factorial ablation across four factors: $\dt$-embedding mode (trained / frozen / none), temporal subsampling (on / off), imagination horizon ($H{=}15$ / $H{=}8$), and imagination step size ($\dt{=}1$, $2$, or $4$). Table~\ref{tab:factorial_design} summarizes the experimental configurations.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Factorial design. Each row specifies one experimental configuration. \textbf{Emb}: $\dt$-embedding mode. \textbf{Sub}: temporal subsampling. \textbf{H}: imagination horizon. \textbf{Imag $\dt$}: temporal resolution used during imagination. Note: 006 enables subsample ON relative to the earlier 005 (learned gate) configuration; all other hyperparameters are shared.}
\label{tab:factorial_design}
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.5pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{ID} & \textbf{Purpose} & \textbf{Emb} & \textbf{Sub} & \textbf{H} & \textbf{Imag $\dt$} \\
\midrule
001 & Reference baseline & --- & --- & 15 & 1 \\
006 & Fixed $\dt{=}2$ & trn & on & 15 & 2 \\
007 & Fixed $\dt{=}4$ & trn & on & 15 & 4 \\
\midrule
008a & Unexposed emb ctrl & exp$^a$ & off & 15 & 2 \\
008c & Horizon control & exp$^a$ & off & 8 & 2 \\
008d & Collapse condition & trn & on & 8 & 2 \\
008e\_v3 & $\dt{=}1$ imag control & trn & on & 15 & 1 \\
008f & Causal ablation & --- & on & 8 & 2 \\
\midrule
015b & Frozen-emb ctrl & frz$^b$ & on & 8 & 2 \\
016e & Stop-grad (reward) & trn$^\dagger$ & on & 8 & 2 \\
018 & Full-SGS & trn$^\ddagger$ & on & 8 & 2 \\
\midrule
005 & Learned gate & adaptive & off & 15 & learned \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{-2pt}
{\raggedright\footnotesize
trn = trained ($d{=}32$, exposed to $\dt{>}1$ data via subsample ON);
exp$^a$ = trained ($d{=}32$) but never exposed to $\dt{>}1$ data (subsample OFF);
frz$^b$ = frozen random projection ($d{=}32$, \texttt{requires\_grad=False});
--- = removed ($d{=}0$).\\
$^\dagger$ \texttt{reward\_dt\_emb\_detach=True}; $^\ddagger$ all non-KL gradients to \texttt{dt\_emb} detached.\par}
\end{table}

The design isolates trained $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure as orthogonal factors, with 008d vs.\ 008f (embedding effect), 008d vs.\ 008a (exposure effect), and 008e\_v3 (imagination-channel control) as key contrasts. Each non-baseline configuration uses 2 seeds; following \citet{agarwal2021precipice}, we report per-seed trajectories to surface stochastic divergence.

\subsection{Diagnostic Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}

\paragraph{Evaluation return.}
The primary performance metric: mean return over 10 evaluation episodes, computed every 10K training steps with a deterministic policy.

\paragraph{Value-to-eval ratio.}
We define the value-to-eval ratio $v/e$ as the ratio of the critic's mean imagined value to the evaluation return at each checkpoint. This tracks how well value estimates match realized performance. A ratio near~1 indicates calibrated value predictions; values substantially above~1 indicate systematic overestimation.

\paragraph{Normalized value-to-eval ratio ($\nve$).}
To control for the baseline's natural $v/e$ dynamics (\eg, the ratio naturally increases during early training as the critic learns), we normalize:
\begin{equation}
\nve_t = \frac{(v/e)_{\text{config},\,t}}{(v/e)_{\text{baseline},\,t}}
\label{eq:nve}
\end{equation}
where $(v/e)_{\text{baseline},\,t}$ is from the single baseline seed (exp\_001, seed~1, 505K steps).  We restrict to a \textbf{35K+ window} ($t \geq 35\text{K}$) because the baseline $v/e$ is near zero in early training, causing spurious $\nve$ inflation.  We use $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ (peak within this window) as a warning threshold; across 12 experimental configurations, this achieves 11/12 accuracy (AUROC${=}0.926$), with one non-CIVO false positive (\S\ref{sec:f6}).  The threshold is sensitive but not perfectly specific to CIVO; mechanistic attribution requires joint analysis with collapse signatures (\S\ref{sec:pathways}).

We distinguish two regimes above the threshold: \textbf{sustained CIVO}, characterized by monotonically escalating $\nve$ culminating in policy collapse (e.g., exp\_008d: $1.78\times \rightarrow 4.12\times$, eval $167 \rightarrow 82.8$), and \textbf{transient exceedance}, where $\nve$ briefly exceeds $1.5\times$ at isolated checkpoints but self-corrects without catastrophic collapse (e.g., exp\_009b: peak $1.62\times$ at 45K, self-correcting to $0.38\times$).  The pathological CIVO diagnosis requires sustained escalation with accompanying performance degradation, not merely a single checkpoint above the threshold.

\paragraph{RSSM fidelity.}
To probe the mechanism underlying value drift, we evaluate world model prediction quality at different temporal scales from the same checkpoint. Specifically, we compute observation MSE and reward MAE on held-out $\dt{=}1$ versus $\dt{=}2$ transitions, and report the gap ratio between them. A large gap indicates that the world model produces systematically different prediction quality across temporal scales; this difference provides the channel for critic exploitation.

\paragraph{Supplementary diagnostics.}
Two additional metrics target failure modes that $\nve$ may miss.
\emph{Actor loss sign}: DreamerV3's actor loss is normally negative (policy improvement direction). A sign flip to positive values directly signals that the critic is misleading the actor and serves as a more targeted marker of delusional-critic damage than the aggregate $\nve$ ratio.
\emph{Value standard deviation}: exponential growth in value variance across imagination rollouts captures oscillatory instability during ``false recovery'' phases where $\nve$ may temporarily decrease even as the underlying value landscape destabilizes.

\subsection{Selective Gradient Stopping}
\label{sec:sgs_method}

To identify which gradient pathway through $e_{\Delta t}$ drives CIVO, we introduce \textit{selective gradient stopping} (SGS): during the backward pass, $e_{\Delta t}$ is detached before entering selected prediction heads via the stop-gradient operator $\operatorname{sg}(\cdot)$, preventing those loss gradients from updating $e_{\Delta t}$'s parameters while preserving its forward-pass contribution.

We test two variants. \textbf{Reward-only SGS} (exp\_016e) detaches $e_{\Delta t}$ from the reward head only: $\hat{r}_t = f_{\text{reward}}(\operatorname{sg}(e_{\Delta t}), h_t, z_t)$, testing whether the reward pathway drives CIVO. \textbf{Full-pathway SGS} (exp\_018) detaches $e_{\Delta t}$ from all non-KL gradient pathways (observation decoders, reward head, and continuation predictor), retaining only the KL dynamics loss signal. To quantify each pathway's contribution, we perform gradient decomposition using \texttt{torch.autograd.grad}, measuring the $\ell_2$ norm of $\nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}_i$ for each loss component $\mathcal{L}_i$, where $\theta$ denotes the temporal embedding parameters.

\subsection{Environment and Training Details}
\label{sec:env_details}

All experiments use Walker Walk and Cheetah Run from the DeepMind Control Suite \citep{tassa2018dmcontrol} with proprioceptive observations. The baseline runs for 500K environment steps, sufficient for convergence (peak return 970.4 on Walker Walk). Treatment configurations run for 105K steps, which is sufficient to observe both collapse onset and the diagnostic markers described above. All DreamerV3 hyperparameters follow the defaults from \citet{hafner2023dreamerv3} without task-specific tuning. The temporal embedding uses dimension $d{=}32$. Training uses a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPU (96\,GB) with JIT compilation disabled (\texttt{-{}-compile False}) for reproducibility; wall-clock time is ${\sim}2.5$h per 105K-step run and ${\sim}12$h for the baseline (${\sim}80$ GPU-hours total). The codebase extends the official DreamerV3 implementation\footnote{\url{https://github.com/danijar/dreamerv3}, accessed March 2026.} with the variable-rate modules described in \S\ref{sec:variable_rate}; no other architectural modifications are made.

